#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188063 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c72f3cc2-5851-5c6b-8edc-74377b88055a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10433" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ a & 5 & b \\\\ c & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ for some random variables $X_1, X_2$, and $X_3$ is given above. Find $a + b + c$." :question/worked-solution "For random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X_{1}] & Cov[X_{1}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{1}, X_{n}] \\\\ Cov[X_{2}, X_{1}] & Var[X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{2}, X_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ Cov[X_{n}, X_{1}] & Cov[X_{n}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Var[X_{n}]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i]$, and\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j]$.\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{Cov}[Y,X]\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix).\n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a = \\Sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = \\Sigma_{12} = 2$\n- $b = \\Sigma_{23} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_2] = \\Sigma_{32} = 1$\n- $c = \\Sigma_{31} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = \\Sigma_{13} = 0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na + b + c = 2 + 1 +0 = 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188070 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46118198-5e82-5b8f-8adc-6f25ecbd9b3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Discrete Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "5b315157-43c6-5b8e-ac70-8b781b41cec9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10452" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 3$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.2$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$." :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$. These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$. If $X$ is defined on a support $S_X$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x^{2} \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot 0.5 + 4^{2} \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 10 - 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support $S_Y$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y^{2}] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y^{2} \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 1^{2} \\cdot 0.4 + 3^{2} \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 5.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= 5.8 - 2.2^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$. By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\textrm{E}[XY]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)xy \\cdot f(x, y) \\\\\n&= (2)(1)f(2, 1) + (4)(1)f(4, 1) + (2)(3)f(2, 3) + (4)(3)f(4, 3) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0.3 + 4 \\cdot 0.1 + 6 \\cdot 0.2 + 12 \\cdot 0.4 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 7 - 3 \\cdot 2.2 \\\\\n&= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188071 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/id #uuid "33336c58-c83a-512f-8a79-9223d1442763" :question/math-academy-id "e-10453" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\textrm{E}[X] = 4$, $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 3$, mixed moment $\\textrm{E}[XY] = 9$, and second raw moments $\\textrm{E}[X^2] = 18$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y^2] = 14$. Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$." :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\textrm{Var}[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 18 - 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= 14 - 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 9 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "3a22ccc6-495b-5b89-8b7d-775e6e620fe0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10454" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n${x + \\frac{3}{2}y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nIf $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}$, $\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac{5}{8}$, and $\\textrm{E}[XY]=\\dfrac{17}{48}$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$." :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\nWe compute $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}(x + \\frac{3}{2}y^{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(x^{3} + \\frac{3}{2}x^{2}y^{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{4}x^{4} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{3}y^{2}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2}y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{4}y + \\frac{1}{6}y^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12} - (\\frac{7}{12})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{144}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\textrm{E}[Y^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}y^{2} \\cdot f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}y^{2}(x + \\frac{3}{2}y^{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(xy^{2} + \\frac{3}{2}y^{4})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y^{2} + \\frac{3}{2}xy^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} + \\frac{3}{2}y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6}y^{3} + \\frac{3}{10}y^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{3}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{15} - (\\frac{5}{8})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{73}{960}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$. Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{48} - (\\frac{7}{12}) \\cdot (\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{96}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc701046-6aa5-574d-a3e1-7fe8534bacb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187021} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbc4b0d-8dc9-567e-851d-58cf505b86e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187022} {:answer/id #uuid "20cba987-cb85-5a6e-a8cd-f57031c51814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187023} {:answer/id #uuid "2acfeb8f-5bc5-581c-b61a-2c23a759020b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187024} {:answer/id #uuid "5317636a-3a97-55c8-a8f3-5fd6e9727715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187025}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4804197d-04ff-5c8c-8cdf-466d734495d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187020}] :question/id #uuid "29c433e1-ba8d-5d7e-b983-459746ebf86b" :question/math-academy-id "q-148179" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $E[X] = 2$, $E[Y] = 4$, mixed moment $E[XY] = 10$, and second raw moments $E[X^{2}] = 9$ and $E[Y^{2}] = 20$. Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be9bb48c-42c8-55d4-b28d-adfbed5aeb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187039} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cf221e-73d0-5de8-ac48-1f15e8e32e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187040} {:answer/id #uuid "4b876341-98a3-5df9-9ab7-6f2de028597c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187041} {:answer/id #uuid "59820e42-d3e1-501a-98a3-420dddfc8214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187042} {:answer/id #uuid "7c403809-fa53-55fb-b496-4c239b372165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187043}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f13c53ef-1e0a-55f0-86d4-e8d2d1425533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187038}] :question/id #uuid "150d719c-c9e6-5c71-9c3c-1c207b1e3bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148189" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$f(x, y) = {x + y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nGiven that $E[X] = E[Y] = \\frac{7}{12}$, and $E[X^{2}] = \\frac{5}{12}$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
